= Program (computing) — Technical =

== Program representation ==
A program may exist as source text, an abstract syntax tree, bytecode, intermediate representation, machine code, database procedure, firmware image, or another representation accepted by an execution system.

The same source program can be transformed through several representations before execution.

== Artifact versus process ==
A program is a persistent or transferable description/artifact. A process is an executing instance.

Conceptually:

<pre>
program + input + environment + runtime state → execution/process behavior
</pre>

This distinction matters for concurrency, reproducibility, security, and operating-system resource management.

== Semantics ==
A program's semantics can be described operationally as transitions, denotationally by mathematical meanings, or axiomatically through logical assertions.

Program equivalence depends on which observations are considered relevant. Two implementations may compute the same final function but differ in timing, I/O, exceptions, resource usage, or interaction.

== Translation pipeline ==
Common stages include:
* lexical analysis and parsing;
* semantic/type analysis;
* intermediate representation;
* optimization;
* code generation;
* linking/loading;
* interpretation or execution;
* runtime services.

Not every language or system uses all stages, and JIT/runtime specialization can interleave translation with execution.

== Correctness ==
Correctness requires a specification.

Formal reasoning may distinguish:
* partial correctness;
* termination;
* safety;
* liveness;
* refinement;
* observational equivalence.

Testing is complementary rather than equivalent to proof.

== Environment dependence ==
Program behavior can depend on undefined/implementation-defined behavior, memory models, scheduling, filesystem state, network responses, locale, dependency versions, hardware, or external services.

Reproducible execution therefore requires more context than source revision alone.

